<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/foundational/ratios-percentages-proportion">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is a beginner lesson in Mathematics, on the foundational track. It assumes
    no prior knowledge beyond everyday arithmetic — we start with a comparison you
    make every time you shop. Every new word is explained the moment it appears.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the species of numbers, and organise them with set language;</li>
    <li>use index laws, roots and standard form for very large and very small numbers;</li>
    <li>split a quantity in a given ratio, and recognise direct and inverse proportion;</li>
    <li>do percentage change, successive changes, and reverse percentages with multipliers;</li>
    <li>model exponential growth and decay, and estimate doubling times.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Start with a comparison</h2>
  <p>
    A stadium holding 60,000 people sounds enormous — until you learn the city
    around it holds six million. Counting tells you <em>how many</em>; comparison
    tells you what the number means. The mathematics of comparison comes in three
    connected forms.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>ratio</strong> is a comparison frozen into a number: 3:2 flour to
    water, one chef to five tables. A <strong>percentage</strong> is just a ratio
    out of 100 — "83%" literally means 83 per cent, 83 per hundred — chosen as a
    common yardstick so that comparisons become easy. And
    <strong>proportion</strong> is the assumption that a ratio stays fixed as
    quantities change: double the flour, double the water; twice the distance,
    twice the fuel. Nearly every headline statistic, shop discount, recipe, map
    scale and exchange rate is one of these three.
  </p>
  <p>
    To handle comparisons well you also need to know what kind of number you are
    comparing with. So this lesson first surveys the number zoo itself, then
    builds up powers and standard form for very large and very small values, and
    finally develops ratios, percentages and proportional reasoning — ending with
    the most dramatic proportional process of all: exponential growth, the
    mathematics of doubling.
  </p>

  <h2>The number zoo, and the sets that organise it</h2>
  <p>
    Numbers come in nested species. The <strong>natural numbers</strong> 1, 2, 3,
    … are the counting numbers; add zero and the negatives and you get the
    <strong>integers</strong>. Ratios of integers — ½, −3, 0.75, any terminating
    or repeating decimal — are the <strong>rational numbers</strong>. But the
    ancient Greeks discovered to their shock that the diagonal of a unit square,
    √2, is <em>not</em> a ratio of any integers: its decimal runs forever without
    repeating. Such numbers are <strong>irrational</strong> (√2, π, e), and
    rationals plus irrationals together form the <strong>real numbers</strong> —
    the complete number line.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the number line is "full" of decimals because
    there are infinitely many fractions between any two of them. True, yet the
    irrationals are <em>more</em> numerous in a precise sense (Cantor proved it in
    1874): if you threw a dart at the number line, it would hit an irrational with
    probability 1. The fractions, infinite as they are, are a dust.
  </p>
  <p>
    This nesting is handled with <strong>set</strong> language. A set is a
    collection with a definite membership, and the number species nest inside
    each other (ℕ ⊂ ℤ ⊂ ℚ ⊂ ℝ) like circles within circles on a Venn diagram.
    <strong>Intersection</strong> (∩) means "in both", <strong>union</strong>
    (∪) means "in either", and the complement means "outside". The vocabulary
    sounds formal but earns its keep the moment you meet overlapping categories —
    "students taking physics ∪ chemistry", or the events that can and cannot
    happen together, which probability (its own lesson later in this course)
    builds on.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> numbers come in nested species — counting numbers,
    integers, rationals, irrationals, reals — and sets give the language for
    sorting anything into overlapping groups.
  </p>

  <h2>Powers and roots: shorthand for repeated multiplication</h2>
  <p>
    Powers exist because repeated multiplication is as common as repeated
    addition. 2⁵ means 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2, and three <strong>index laws</strong>
    do all the algebra: multiplying powers <strong>adds</strong> indices
    (2³ × 2² = 2⁵), dividing <strong>subtracts</strong> them (2⁵ ÷ 2² = 2³), and
    a power of a power <strong>multiplies</strong> them ((2²)³ = 2⁶) — each law
    is just counting the multiplications.
  </p>
  <p>
    The definitions that fall out are beautiful. The zero index: a⁰ = 1, so that
    dividing a³ by a³ gives a⁰ = 1. Negative indices are reciprocals: 2⁻¹ = ½.
    Fractional indices are roots: 2^(1/2) = √2, because squaring it returns 2.
    <strong>Roots</strong> are the inverse operation — √81 = 9 asks "9 × what =
    81" — and squares and square roots are pairs, like packing and unpacking.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a negative index makes a negative number. It
    does not: 2⁻³ = 1/8, a small positive number — the minus sign sends you below
    1, never below 0.
  </p>

  <h2>Standard form: taming the astronomical</h2>
  <p>
    Science refuses to write 0.000000000000000000000021 g or 300,000,000 m/s by
    hand, and <strong>standard form</strong> (scientific notation) is the fix:
    every number written as A × 10ⁿ, with A at least 1 and under 10. The speed of
    light is 3 × 10⁸ m/s; the mass of a hydrogen atom is 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg. The
    notation is really applied index laws: multiplying (3 × 10⁸) × (2 × 10⁻¹⁰)
    multiplies the As and <em>adds</em> the indices, giving 6 × 10⁻².
  </p>
  <p>
    Worked example: a virus is 1.2 × 10⁻⁷ m long and a red blood cell is
    7.5 × 10⁻⁶ m. The ratio is (7.5 ÷ 1.2) × 10⁻⁶⁻⁽⁻⁷⁾ = 6.25 × 10¹ = 62.5 — so
    about 63 viruses would span a blood cell. Without the notation, that
    comparison is an error factory.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> index laws turn repeated multiplication into
    arithmetic, and standard form stretches them across thirty powers of ten —
    from viruses to planets.
  </p>

  <h2>Ratio and proportion: the mathematics of recipes</h2>
  <p>
    A ratio like 3 : 2 is a <em>statement of structure</em>: for every 3 parts of
    one thing, 2 of another. Its power comes from the <strong>unitary
    method</strong> — find one part, scale to any total. Concrete case: a
    concrete mix of 1 : 2 : 3 (cement : sand : gravel) for 240 kg total means 6
    parts, so one part is 40 kg — giving 40, 80 and 120 kg respectively.
  </p>
  <p>
    Change the total and the ratios never move. That is <strong>direct
    proportion</strong>, written y ∝ x, and it is the mathematics behind currency
    exchange, map scales (1 : 50,000 means 1 cm on the map is 50,000 cm in the
    field), and the price of anything sold by weight. <strong>Inverse
    proportion</strong> is its mirror: more workers, fewer days. If 6 builders
    finish in 12 days, then 9 builders take 6 × 12 ÷ 9 = 8 days, because what
    stays fixed is the <em>product</em> (36 builder-days of work), not the ratio.
    Mixing the two up — assuming more workers means longer, or that halving the
    recipe halves the cooking time — is the classic trap. The honest test is
    always: <em>what quantity is actually constant?</em>
  </p>
  <p>
    Ratios, fractions, decimals and percentages are one language in four
    alphabets, and fluency means translating instantly: 3 : 5 as a share of a
    whole is 3/8 and 5/8 of it, or 37.5% and 62.5%. A worked share: prize money
    of £600 split 2 : 3 : 5 has 10 parts, so one part is £60 — £120, £180 and
    £300, whose sum checks back to £600. Simplifying uses the same move as
    simplifying fractions — divide by common factors (12 : 18 = 2 : 3) — and
    comparing ratios needs a common second term (3 : 4 versus 5 : 7 becomes
    21 : 28 versus 20 : 28, so 3 : 4 is the steeper ratio).
  </p>
  <p>
    One warning from the kitchen: a ratio comparison only makes sense between
    quantities of the same kind. 3 eggs to 2 cups of flour is a recipe, not a
    ratio to be scaled against 3 eggs to 2 cups of sugar — and mixing "parts of"
    different totals is the commonest source of ratio errors in exams and in
    real catering alike.
  </p>
  <Widget no="1" caption="percentage change, computed honestly — drag both values and watch the baseline do the work" widget="sliderLab" config={pctCfg} />

  <h2>Percentages: ratios with a shared yardstick</h2>
  <p>
    Percentages are ratios normalised to 100, and every percentage calculation
    reduces to one move: the <strong>multiplier</strong>. A 20% increase is
    ×1.20; a 20% discount is ×0.80; two successive changes <em>multiply</em> —
    up 10% then down 10% is ×1.1 × 0.9 = ×0.99, a net 1% <em>loss</em>, because
    the second 10% is taken of a bigger base.
  </p>
  <p>
    As the widget shows, <strong>percentage change always divides by the old
    value</strong>, so the answer depends on which direction you travel: a rise
    from 100 to 150 is +50%, but the fall from 150 back to 100 is −33%. "Up 50%,
    down 50%" does not return to the start — percentages compound, they do not
    add. The <strong>reverse percentage</strong> problem — a coat costs £84 after
    a 30% discount, what was the original? — is solved by running the multiplier
    backwards: 84 ÷ 0.70 = £120, <em>not</em> 84 + 30% of 84 = £109.20. Finding
    the original base is the single most-asked percentage question in shops, tax
    and pay rises alike.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "percentage points and per cent are the same." If a bank
    rate rises from 2% to 3%, that is one <strong>percentage point</strong> — but a
    <strong>50% increase</strong> in the rate. News reports swap these constantly, and the
    difference between "unemployment fell 2%" and "fell 2 percentage points" can be the whole
    story. Every percentage needs a named baseline: "50% faster" than what? A percentage
    without a stated base is a rumour.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> ratios split and scale through the unitary method,
    direct and inverse proportion hinge on what stays constant, and percentages
    are the same idea in a shared yardstick of 100.
  </p>

  <h2>Exponential growth: proportion taken to the limit</h2>
  <p>
    The deepest proportional process is the one where the <em>ratio per step</em>
    is constant: each period multiplies by the same factor. That is
    <strong>exponential growth</strong>. Money at 7% a year becomes A = P ×
    1.07ⁿ after n years; a population of 1,000 bacteria doubling every 20 minutes
    becomes 1,000 × 2ⁿ. The signature is <strong>equal ratios in equal
    times</strong>, so the graph curves upward ever more steeply.
  </p>
  <p>
    The useful intuition is <strong>doubling time</strong>: the "rule of 72" says
    money at r% interest doubles in roughly 72/r years — at 7.2% a year, about a
    decade. The mirror process, <strong>exponential decay</strong>, multiplies by
    a constant factor below 1: a drug's concentration halving every 4 hours, or
    carbon-14 decaying by half every ~5,730 years — radiocarbon dating is reverse
    percentage change with a very old baseline.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "exponential" means "very fast". It means
    <em>constant-ratio</em>: it starts slow — a doubling from 1 to 2 looks trivial — and the
    scary part is that thirty doublings later the number is a billion. The lily-pond that
    fills on day 30 was half empty on day 29.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A concrete mix uses cement : sand : gravel = 1 : 2 : 3. How much sand is in 300 kg of mix?</li>
    <li>A jumper costs £48 after a 20% discount. What was its original price?</li>
    <li>A bacteria culture starts at 500 and doubles every hour. How many bacteria are there after 4 hours?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) The mix has 1 + 2 + 3 = 6 parts, so one part is
    300 ÷ 6 = 50 kg; sand is 2 parts, so 100 kg. (2) The multiplier for the
    discounted price is 0.80, so the original was 48 ÷ 0.80 = £60 — check:
    60 × 0.80 = 48. (3) Each hour multiplies by 2, so after 4 hours the count is
    500 × 2⁴ = 500 × 16 = 8,000.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The expert layer connects the topics you have just met into one mechanism. Exponential
    growth is where indices, ratios and percentages fuse: "grows by 7% per year" <em>is</em>
    the index law A = P × 1.07ⁿ, and undoing it — "how long until it doubles?" — is a
    logarithm question (n = log 2 ÷ log 1.07 ≈ 10.2), the logarithm being precisely the
    inverse of the exponential. This is why a slide rule, a pH scale and an earthquake
    magnitude are all logarithms in disguise: they exist to turn <em>multiplicative</em>
    comparisons into <em>additive</em> ones, the same translation you already perform every
    time you write 10⁶ instead of 1,000,000.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two nuances separate the fluent from the merely trained. First,
    <strong>compounding frequency matters</strong>: £1,000 at 12% once a year becomes £1,120,
    but at 1% a month it becomes 1,000 × 1.01¹² ≈ £1,126.8 — and as compounding gets
    continuous the multiplier approaches e ≈ 2.71828, the natural base, the special rate
    where growth equals the current amount. Second,
    <strong>proportional reasoning is the check, not just the method</strong>: before any
    computation, ask what the answer should do when an input doubles. Fuel cost should double
    with distance (direct), time with the number of pages (direct), but not stopping distance
    with speed (it grows as the square of the speed). Innumeracy, as
    the mathematician John Allen Paulos put it, is mostly the failure to run these quick
    proportionality sanity checks; numeracy is having them on reflex.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Prealgebra &amp; Elementary Algebra; Paulos, Innumeracy; Cambridge IGCSE Mathematics syllabus (E1).</p>
